#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331939 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc0250e-2aa9-5439-b567-93a59fa1c364" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Term In the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e28d33c6-9b54-5018-8c30-20b4328565a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331941} {:answer/id #uuid "f64f7624-f5e2-5746-81d4-517943568d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331942} {:answer/id #uuid "377d4d07-1be2-5ae5-a838-0cd5db52bc19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331943} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab1012d-433c-5c61-b762-c95a6cfddbbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331944} {:answer/id #uuid "aab72c3b-3e42-516f-81fc-8d253ba110c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "377d4d07-1be2-5ae5-a838-0cd5db52bc19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331943} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd34680-796d-5b2d-a195-fe32a22dd5ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "384d449f-273e-593f-9d4d-a9fdf55ff2d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126359" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}$ about $x=0?$" :question/worked-solution "The coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(0).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{6}{(1-x)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(0),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(0)=\\frac{6}{(1-0)^{4}}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(0)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6=1.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc0250e-2aa9-5439-b567-93a59fa1c364" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Term In the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49bce016-19b0-5418-a632-3e4a4a465f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331948} {:answer/id #uuid "71187d4a-22f1-599d-99ed-a5664a0f98f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331949} {:answer/id #uuid "b42176ef-174e-5805-88ab-84857c192b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331950} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0ca27e-b43a-59d9-b50d-713673aeff1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331951} {:answer/id #uuid "42273e68-e32b-54b0-b2af-9d895f8d8bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42273e68-e32b-54b0-b2af-9d895f8d8bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331952} :answer-field/id #uuid "8101ebe3-8ace-5fc9-9b0f-a5d20dd4fce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79a56d13-19ce-5345-b6e6-3de7b06f4714" :question/math-academy-id "q-15479" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ about $x=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(-1).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{24}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(-1),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(-1)=-\\frac{24}{(-1)^{5}}=-\\frac{24}{(-1)}=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-1)=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot 24=\\frac{24}{6}=4.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x+1)^{3}$ is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dc0250e-2aa9-5439-b567-93a59fa1c364" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Term In the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8847414c-3149-580c-8e29-1b7864bad685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098467} {:answer/id #uuid "f47ecc3e-349f-5f82-8047-8f1810bbfbbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098468} {:answer/id #uuid "17a61029-19d2-5ceb-8480-f7630f8dee73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098469} {:answer/id #uuid "1ddeaf47-a449-5e39-9d5a-a798c1b57def" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098470} {:answer/id #uuid "69beeed6-d55e-502d-a7c5-74f4154a9612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f47ecc3e-349f-5f82-8047-8f1810bbfbbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098468} :answer-field/id #uuid "92710c14-72a9-5088-a428-1e89e02ac5d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a5c836a-5f7e-5ef1-b659-e3eaf7b89ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15484" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ about $x=-\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(-\\pi ).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{‴}(-\\pi ),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{‴}(-\\pi )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{‴}(-\\pi )=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ea3dac-cf0a-5d05-8d77-7280ce9b5a94" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial For a Given Function About a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba2eba6-98da-551a-a9d7-eaba0126d0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^2-\\frac{4}{3}x^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba2eba6-98da-551a-a9d7-eaba0126d0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^2-\\frac{4}{3}x^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331955} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a61a26-d697-50e5-9a14-f09ca5b0e334" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "783fb36c-345a-5c94-9d91-f565c09ac763" :question/math-academy-id "q-351859" :question/problem "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}(x)$ of $f(x)={e}^{-2x}$ about $x=0.$\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=1-2x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!} \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-2x} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & ={e}^{-0}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2{e}^{-2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-2{e}^{-0}=-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{-2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =4{e}^{-0}=4 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-8{e}^{-2x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(0) & =-8{e}^{-0}=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =1-2\\cdot x+4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =1-2x+2{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ea3dac-cf0a-5d05-8d77-7280ce9b5a94" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Third-Degree Taylor Polynomial For a Given Function About a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fce4228c-cfb9-5019-80e2-cc10096c0284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fce4228c-cfb9-5019-80e2-cc10096c0284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331958} :answer-field/id #uuid "e10cb6b6-68f5-50a6-9f85-97dc918b5cc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55dfc2a7-a4a6-59e8-afc2-ff10a44550da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55dfc2a7-a4a6-59e8-afc2-ff10a44550da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331960} :answer-field/id #uuid "c856bb87-8e3b-5471-8c50-c413f3aba9de" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331959} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee32e8c8-f24c-58ef-bd1b-19c51dfa1a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee32e8c8-f24c-58ef-bd1b-19c51dfa1a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331962} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6a6fde1-b724-52d0-849e-f48493caa0a0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da23cd02-a4af-5514-bd1d-fc11e7c72462" :question/math-academy-id "q-351980" :question/problem "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ about $x=0.$ Do  not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n${x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!} \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(0) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results into our Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}x+(-\\frac{1}{2})\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{12}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
